--- a/solver/noframe/docs/proofs.md
+++ b/solver/noframe/docs/proofs.md
@@ -283,3 +283,44 @@ By band, the open members and the margins of each game (the share is of all memb
   measured before; no game is flagged.
 * **The open members are mostly of layer 3** (52% to 2175 and 58% past it), then of layer 5 (21%, 27%) and 4 (19%, 9%),
   and the rest in layers 6 to 11.
+
+### Forcing the certified members
+
+`exact --fix-certified 1` (off by default; it needs the roles of F4, and is refused with `--fix-upper 1`) starts its
+search from the roles of F4 with every certified member forced as well.  The search is a proof like the one from the
+roles of F4 alone: its lines say `"search":"proven"` and also `"fix_certified":1`.
+
+* **The rule.**  Every set that beats the target V and obeys F4 holds each certified member.  So if some set beats V,
+  an optimal set does, it obeys F4, and it holds every certified member; a search over the sets that obey F4 and hold
+  them all therefore still decides whether any set beats V.
+* **The iteration.**  With the certified members forced, `upper::certify` bars each member still open again.  Its bound
+  can only be lower, because the forced members restrict the sets, so more members might be certified, and by the same
+  argument they are forced too.  It repeats the round until one certifies nothing.  Every round rests only on "every set
+  that beats V and obeys F4 holds the members certified so far", never on the search, so the roles it returns keep the
+  search a proof.  A bound that is not certified (`Uncertified`) leaves a member open.
+* **What the iteration finds.**  With the parity bound, nothing after the first round.  The bound is the exact maximum
+  over the sets independent at both parities, so a heaviest set with an open member barred, worth more than V, lacks no
+  certified member c: a set that lacks c is worth at most V.  Forcing the certified members therefore leaves the bound
+  of every open member where it was, and the second round certifies nothing.  The tests check it on boards 2..60, and
+  `upper --iterate 1` found no member beyond the first round's on the 61 games of 1301-1400 that need it.
+* **What the search does by itself.**  Its propagation at every node, the root included, forces each free member of the
+  parity bound's heaviest set whose barring alone brings the bound to the target, and repeats until nothing more is
+  forced (the forced picks of the search, above).  That is the certification at the root, so a search started from the
+  forced roles starts where the search from the roles of F4 arrives after the root.  On games 1309, 1508 and 1920 the
+  two searches took the same numbers of nodes.
+* **The target.**  A certificate is relative to the target: a member certified against a higher score says nothing of
+  the sets worth more than a lower one.  `exact` certifies against the target it searches, the recorded score.
+* **The checks.**  The recorded game plays all of U\*, so it holds every forced member and obeys the roles; `exact`
+  checks that, and that the parity bound at the roles is at least the score (`fixed_bound`).
+* **The lines.**  Besides `"fix_certified":1`, a game that was searched has `certified` (the members forced),
+  `members` (those of U\*_{≥3}), `certify_rounds` (the rounds of barring, the last of which certified nothing) and
+  `certify_secs`, which `secs` includes; a game that was not searched has them all 0.  The checkpoints of this kind are
+  `exact_N_cert.ckpt`, and `tools/summarize.py exact` and `check` count the lines as proofs, as they count those from
+  the roles of F4.
+* **`upper --iterate 1`** writes the same certification game by game: its `certified` and `open` are those after the
+  last round, and the line adds `rounds` and `certified_first`, the members the first round certified.
+
+The tests check it against every playable set that obeys F4, on the boards and claims of the tests above: the members
+certified in rounds are held by every set worth more than the claim, the iterated wrong rule (certify when the bound is
+at most T + 10) is caught by the same check, and a search from the forced roles proves the score on games that need a
+search and finds a game worth the score from one below it.
